    {
      "id": "r20261112-a02",
      "title": "Why Cities Are Turning Down Their Lights",
      "level": "Menengah",
      "min": 3,
      "body": [
        "On a clear night in a big city, you might count ten stars. In the countryside, you could count thousands. The difference is light pollution, and some cities have decided to fight it.",
        "Several towns now dim their streetlights after midnight and ask shops to turn off bright signs. The results surprise everyone: people sleep better, migrating birds stop crashing into buildings, and the night sky slowly returns.",
        "The movement is not only about beauty. Too much light at night confuses animals and wastes energy. \"We forgot the stars were ours too,\" says one astronomer. \"Now we are turning down the lights to get them back.\""
      ],
      "gloss": {
        "clear": "cerah",
        "pollution": "polusi",
        "dim": "meredupkan",
        "signs": "papan reklame",
        "migrating": "bermigrasi",
        "astronomer": "astronom"
      },
      "qs": [
        {
          "q": "Why can people see more stars in the countryside?",
          "opts": [
            "The air is colder",
            "The stars are closer",
            "There is less light pollution"
